{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Toney homes — especially older ones — have downspout problems they don't realize. Original downspouts may be undersized, positioned poorly, or simply not enough of them for the roof area.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't keep up with heavy rain, water backs up in gutters and overflows. Even worse, many downspouts dump water right at the foundation. On Toney's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb — it pools and eventually finds its way into your home.</p>

<p>Rural properties can make this worse. Without municipal drainage, water has nowhere to go except where gravity takes it. If that's toward your foundation, you've got a problem that properly designed downspouts can solve.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective extensions.</p>

<p>Capacity means enough downspouts, sized correctly for your roof and North Alabama's heavy rainfall. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 — they handle storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally collects. Extensions (or underground drains) carry that water far from your foundation, not just a few inches past the edge.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been solving drainage problems on Toney properties for over 20 years. We understand rural challenges: clay soil, lack of municipal drainage, grading issues, and the need to protect foundations on properties that may have stood for generations.</p>

<p>Our downspout solutions are designed for your specific property, not generic one-size-fits-all approaches.</p>
{% endset %}
